"""Original A+ technical reference chapters; examples are authored, not copied exam items."""
import json
from pathlib import Path
lessons=[]
def lesson(topic,key,title,explanation,distinctions,reference,case,correct,wrong,reason,case2,correct2,wrong2,reason2,lab,sources,related=None):
 lessons.append(dict(topic=topic,key=key,title=title,explanation=explanation,distinctions=distinctions,reference=[dict(term=t,meaning=d) for t,d in reference],example=dict(situation=case,reasoning=reason,result=correct),diagram=[dict(label=x) for x in distinctions],lab=lab,related=related or [],questions=[dict(id='depth:'+key+':1',prompt=case,options=[correct]+wrong,answer=0,explanation=reason,tier=3),dict(id='depth:'+key+':2',prompt=case2,options=[correct2]+wrong2,answer=0,explanation=reason2,tier=3)],sources=[dict(title=t,url=u) for t,u in sources]))
MS=('Windows technical reference','https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/client-management/')
CMD=('Microsoft command reference','https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/windows-commands')
APPLE=('Apple Mac user guide','https://support.apple.com/guide/mac-help/welcome/mac')
LINUX=('Linux manual pages','https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/')
MEM=('Crucial memory installation guidance','https://www.crucial.com/support/memory-install-guide')

lesson('core1-1.1','mobile-service','Mobile service boundaries, battery and antennas',
 'Begin with the exact device model and service instructions. Mobile parts may be soldered, adhesively secured, paired to firmware or not intended for field replacement. A laptop RAM socket does not imply that its processor or flash storage is replaceable. Before opening the case, protect data, shut down, disconnect external power and follow the manufacturer’s battery-disconnection and ESD procedure. A swollen or damaged battery is a hazard: stop charging and use the approved handling route rather than squeezing the enclosure shut. Antenna leads are small coaxial connections; route and reconnect them according to their labels and the service guide, because poor placement or a loose connector can degrade radio performance without causing an obvious device-manager error. Cameras, microphones, fingerprint readers and NFC hardware also have privacy or policy controls. Separate physical connection, driver recognition, application permission and organizational policy before deciding a component has failed.',
 ['Model-specific serviceability','Safe power and ESD handling','Physical connection versus software permission','Post-service verification'],
 [('SO-DIMM or soldered RAM','Inspect model documentation; portable size does not guarantee a replaceable module.'),('Swollen battery','Stop use and charging; follow authorized handling and disposal procedures.'),('Antenna MAIN/AUX routing','Follow the labeled model-specific layout; avoid pinching fragile coaxial leads.'),('Webcam/microphone','Check hardware privacy switches, OS permissions and application selection as well as the connection.'),('Fingerprint/NFC','Confirm enrollment, compatible hardware and access policy separately from physical repair.')],
 'A webcam appears in Device Manager and works in one application but not a conferencing app. What should be checked before replacing the module?', 'Application camera selection and camera privacy permissions', ['The entire motherboard must be replaced','Reinstall the operating system before checking settings'], 'Successful use in another app is evidence that the module and some driver path work. The failing application and its permissions are a narrower boundary.',
 'After replacing a wireless card, Wi-Fi connects only near the access point. Which service check is most relevant?', 'Verify the compatible card and the labeled antenna connections/routing', ['Assume any radio connection proves the antenna path is correct','Erase all user data to repair radio range'], 'A damaged, misrouted or disconnected antenna can produce weak range while the radio still associates nearby.',
 ['On a service-manual diagram, label battery isolation and replaceable modules.','Identify antenna connectors and routing without opening a live device.','Build a camera fault tree: module → driver → privacy policy → app selection.','Record one verification test after a simulated repair.'],[MEM,MS],['core1-5.4'])

lesson('core1-1.2','mobile-connectors','Ports, accessories and advertised capabilities',
 'Identify a connector, then separately establish the supported protocol, data rate, power delivery and alternate modes. USB-C is a reversible connector; a particular port or cable may support only a subset of charging, USB data, DisplayPort alternate mode or Thunderbolt capabilities. A charging-only cable can fit a data port and still fail the desired job. Check the laptop’s supported power input and the charger/cable’s negotiated capability; a high wattage printed on one component does not prove the complete path can supply it. A port replicator typically exposes existing connections; a dock may add hubs, display processing, networking and power features. Display resolution, refresh rate and the number of simultaneous monitors are constrained by the host, dock, cable and protocol. Pairing an accessory also differs from connecting by cable: Bluetooth needs discoverability, the correct device and successful authorization; NFC is short-range interaction rather than a general replacement for Wi-Fi throughput.',
 ['Connector shape','Data/video protocol','Cable and device capabilities','Power negotiation'],
 [('USB-A/USB-B','Traditional host/peripheral connector families; shape is not a complete speed specification.'),('MiniUSB/MicroUSB','Older small connector families found on legacy peripherals and devices.'),('USB-C','Connector that can carry multiple supported modes; verify each device and cable.'),('Thunderbolt','A high-speed interface whose generation, certification and host support must be checked.'),('Lightning','Apple connector used on supported older devices; verify model and cable support.'),('Stylus/digitizer','Active stylus functions depend on compatible digitizer technology, not merely a touch surface.'),('Bluetooth headset','Pairing, selected input/output profile, battery and application audio selection all affect operation.'),('Tethering/hotspot','Share a device’s connection by supported USB, Wi-Fi or Bluetooth methods; account for carrier policy and data caps.')],
 'A dock charges a laptop but its monitors stay blank. What evidence is needed before buying another dock?', 'The host port, cable, dock and display modes support the required video path', ['A matching USB-C plug proves full video support','More charging wattage always enables video'], 'Power and video are separate negotiated capabilities. Every component in the video path must support the requested mode.',
 'A passive stylus moves the pointer, but pressure sensitivity does not work. What should support investigate?', 'Whether the stylus protocol, digitizer and app support active pressure features', ['Assume all touchscreens support every active stylus','Replace the network card'], 'Basic capacitive contact does not establish an active stylus protocol or application capability.',
 ['Create a specification matrix for one laptop, cable, dock and two monitors.','Record connector, data mode, display limits and required charging power separately.','Describe a test that distinguishes bad cable, unsupported host mode and wrong display input.','Use only model-specific documented capabilities.'],[MS,APPLE])

lesson('core1-1.3','mobile-connectivity','Cellular, pairing, location and managed synchronization',
 'Separate the radio connection, internet access, account authentication and application synchronization. A phone can have strong cellular signal yet be unable to use a corporate application because its identity, license or management enrollment is not valid. SIM and eSIM identify a subscriber profile; provisioning, carrier compatibility and plan status still determine usable service. Wi-Fi and cellular are different transports, and hotspot use may have a separate allowance. Bluetooth setup usually requires enabling the radio, discoverability or pairing mode, selecting the intended device, confirming the requested code and testing the required function. Location may use satellite positioning, cellular infrastructure, Wi-Fi-related information and OS services; indoor operation and privacy settings affect results. An MDM policy can control configuration, applications and corporate data differently for BYOD and company-owned devices. Synchronization settings specify what moves, which direction and which account owns it. A sync icon is not proof that every item has reached an independent recoverable copy.',
 ['Radio and subscriber profile','IP connectivity','Identity and management policy','Application synchronization'],
 [('SIM/eSIM','Physical or electronically provisioned subscriber identity profile; not a universal guarantee of carrier access.'),('3G/4G/5G','Cellular generations with availability and support dependent on carrier, location and device.'),('Bluetooth pairing','Establish and authorize the intended relationship, then test its actual function.'),('GPS/cellular location','Different sources can contribute to location; privacy controls and environment matter.'),('BYOD/corporate enrollment','Ownership and management policy affect allowed configuration and data separation.'),('Mail/calendar/contacts/cloud sync','Verify account, scope, timing, capacity and conflicts for each data type.')],
 'A phone browses normally but work mail stopped after a password change. What is the best next boundary to investigate?', 'The work account’s authentication, enrollment and synchronization error', ['Replace the cellular antenna first','Assume every internet connection guarantees valid application credentials'], 'Working internet narrows the transport question; account and policy evidence can explain application-specific failure.',
 'A user pairs with a nearby device of an unexpected name and is asked for a PIN. What should happen?', 'Verify the intended accessory identity and pairing procedure before authorizing it', ['Approve every nearby request','Publish the device PIN in a support forum'], 'Pairing is an authorization action; proximity alone does not identify the correct peripheral.',
 ['Draw separate radio, IP, identity and sync states for a phone.','Create a fictional BYOD policy with permitted data and applications.','Describe how to verify a calendar item reached the intended account.','Record data-cap and roaming considerations before a large sync.'],[MS,APPLE])

lesson('core1-2.1','ports-and-transports','Service ports and what a successful connection proves',
 'A port number identifies a transport endpoint convention; it does not prove the service is genuine, secure or configured on that default. TCP supplies an ordered byte stream with connection establishment and retransmission. UDP sends datagrams without that same delivery/order guarantee; an application may add its own reliability. DNS commonly uses UDP for ordinary queries and TCP where required, so calling it UDP-only is incorrect. DHCPv4 uses UDP server/client ports 67/68. When diagnosing access, identify client and server, transport, destination port, actual service configuration, route, stateful or stateless filtering and the return path. A firewall change should permit the needed service as narrowly as practicable rather than open every port. An open TCP port is evidence of a listener or intermediary response, not proof of a working login or correct application behavior. Unencrypted legacy protocols should not be confused with encrypted alternatives just because their purpose is similar.',
 ['Service requirement','Transport and destination port','Forward and return path','Application verification'],
 [('FTP 20/21','21 is control; traditional active-mode data commonly uses 20, while passive operation uses negotiated data ports.'),('SSH 22 / Telnet 23','Remote command-line access; SSH provides encryption, whereas Telnet normally does not.'),('SMTP 25','A common mail-server transport port; submission and protected configurations can use other ports.'),('DNS 53','Name-service queries and related traffic may use UDP and TCP.'),('DHCPv4 67/68','UDP server/client service for address configuration.'),('HTTP 80 / HTTPS 443','Common web ports; HTTPS uses transport protection when correctly configured.'),('POP3 110 / IMAP 143','Common legacy mail-access ports; verify protected alternatives or negotiated protection.'),('NetBIOS 137–139 / SMB 445','Legacy NetBIOS services versus direct-hosted SMB, with transport depending on the specific service.'),('LDAP 389 / RDP 3389','Directory-access and remote-desktop conventions; assess actual security and transport settings.')],
 'A firewall allows UDP 53 but blocks TCP 53. A DNS problem appears during a response requiring TCP. What is the best conclusion?', 'The DNS policy may be incomplete; DNS can require TCP as well as UDP', ['DNS can never use TCP','Opening unrelated RDP traffic fixes DNS by definition'], 'A protocol’s common usage must not be simplified into a false transport restriction.',
 'A TCP connection to port 22 succeeds but SSH authentication fails. What does the connection test establish?', 'A transport path responds; the application identity and authentication still need verification', ['The password is necessarily correct','The target operating system has been fully verified'], 'Transport reachability and application readiness are different outcomes.',
 ['Make a source/destination/transport/port table for web, DNS, DHCP and remote administration.','Trace the return path for one permitted session.','Compare a successful socket connection with a successful authenticated application operation.','Document why broad allow-all rules are an inappropriate diagnostic endpoint.'],[CMD])

lesson('core1-2.2','wireless-basics','Wi-Fi bands, standards, channels and interference',
 'Radio design trades range, spectrum availability, interference, channel width and client capability. The 2.4 GHz band commonly reaches through obstacles better but is crowded and has limited non-overlapping channel choices. The 5 GHz and 6 GHz bands offer additional spectrum where permitted and supported; higher frequency does not automatically mean greater range. Channel widths increase potential throughput but consume more spectrum and can worsen contention in a dense deployment. Regional rules and device capabilities determine valid channels and power. Wi-Fi 4 corresponds to 802.11n, Wi-Fi 5 to 802.11ac and Wi-Fi 6 to 802.11ax; Wi-Fi 6E extends 802.11ax operation into 6 GHz on compatible equipment. A client and access point must both support the desired band and mode. Bluetooth shares parts of the 2.4 GHz environment. NFC operates at very short range, while RFID describes broader identification systems with technology-dependent range. None of these labels alone proves usable application throughput.',
 ['Client/AP capability','Permitted band and channel','Interference and signal quality','Measured application performance'],
 [('802.11a','Legacy 5 GHz Wi-Fi.'),('802.11b/g','Legacy 2.4 GHz Wi-Fi families.'),('802.11n / Wi-Fi 4','Supports 2.4 and/or 5 GHz depending on implementation.'),('802.11ac / Wi-Fi 5','5 GHz Wi-Fi generation.'),('802.11ax / Wi-Fi 6, 6E','Wi-Fi 6 uses supported 2.4/5 GHz operation; 6E adds 6 GHz capability.'),('Channel width','Spectrum consumed by a channel; a wider channel is not automatically better in a crowded area.'),('NFC/RFID','Short-range interaction and radio identification systems respectively; use the specific device standards.')],
 'A 6E-capable access point is installed, but an older 5 GHz-only laptop cannot see its 6 GHz-only network. What is the issue?', 'The client lacks the required band capability', ['An SSID name automatically adds radio hardware','The laptop DNS server determines which RF bands it can receive'], 'Radio support is a hardware/driver/regulatory capability, separate from naming and DNS.',
 'A crowded office becomes less stable after every AP is set to the widest channel. What should be reviewed?', 'Channel reuse, interference, width and client density using a survey', ['Assume more width always improves every client','Disable all authentication to change RF congestion'], 'Wide channels reduce reuse opportunities and can increase contention; deployment evidence should guide the plan.',
 ['Draw a channel plan with assumptions about density and permitted bands.','Compare narrower versus wider channels in that plan.','List client capability checks.','Separate a radio association test from an internet and application test.'],[('Wi-Fi Alliance technology overview','https://www.wi-fi.org/discover-wi-fi')],['core1-5.5'])

lesson('core1-2.4','dns-dhcp-network-config','DNS records, DHCP leases and logical separation',
 'DNS records have distinct roles: A maps a name to IPv4, AAAA to IPv6, CNAME provides an alias, MX identifies mail exchangers and TXT stores text used by mechanisms such as domain verification and email authentication. SPF describes permitted sending sources, DKIM provides a domain-associated message signature, and DMARC evaluates aligned authentication with policy/reporting; these are complementary rather than interchangeable. DHCP scopes define a pool and options, exclusions prevent allocation from reserved portions, and reservations associate a predictable lease with a client identifier. A lease has a lifecycle; a reservation is not simply any manually configured static address. VLANs divide logical layer-2 broadcast domains, while routing and policy determine permitted inter-VLAN traffic. A VPN protects or establishes a communication path according to its design; it does not automatically grant every application permission. Troubleshooting should separately test link, address, gateway, resolver, destination and application behavior.',
 ['Client link','Lease and address options','Gateway/routing policy','DNS record and application'],
 [('A/AAAA','Name to IPv4/IPv6 address records.'),('CNAME','Alias to another name; not a separate IP-address field.'),('MX','Mail exchanger selection, with preference values.'),('TXT/SPF/DKIM/DMARC','Text data and distinct email authentication/policy functions.'),('Scope/exclusion/reservation','Allocation pool, withheld addresses and client-specific lease assignment.'),('VLAN/VPN','Logical local-network separation versus a configured virtual communication path.')],
 'A server should keep the same DHCP-managed address while other clients use a pool. Which configuration fits?', 'A documented DHCP reservation within the addressing design', ['An undocumented static address chosen inside the dynamic pool','A CNAME record as the only address assignment method'], 'Reservations provide predictable DHCP assignment; conflicting static use can create duplicate addresses.',
 'A mail domain publishes SPF but has no DKIM signing or DMARC policy. What should be understood?', 'SPF alone does not supply all the functions of signing, aligned policy and reporting', ['SPF and DKIM are identical encryption mechanisms','A TXT record itself guarantees all mail is genuine'], 'Email authentication mechanisms address different checks and require correct configuration and interpretation.',
 ['Build a fictional DNS zone with one example of each record type.','Draw a DHCP pool with an exclusion and reservation without overlapping assignments.','Trace a client’s address acquisition and name lookup.','Explain a VLAN access decision separately from DNS and VPN identity.'],[CMD],['core1-2.3','core1-2.6','core2-1.7'])

lesson('core1-2.5','network-device-boundaries','Appliances, PoE, physical termination and WAN access',
 'Identify what a device actually does rather than treating every network box as a router. A switch forwards local Ethernet frames; a router forwards packets between IP networks. A wireless access point bridges wireless clients into a network. A firewall permits or denies traffic according to policy, although products often combine several functions. A patch panel is passive cable termination, not a packet-forwarding security device. A modem or access device matches a provider’s physical technology, such as coaxial cable or DSL; fiber services may use an optical network terminal. Power over Ethernet requires a compatible power-sourcing device, powered device, cable plant, per-port capability and total power budget. PoE injectors add power where appropriate switches do not supply it. Managed switches offer configuration and monitoring capabilities that an unmanaged switch typically lacks. WAN choice also involves availability, latency, bandwidth, usage limits and shared failure dependencies, not just the highest advertised download speed.',
 ['Media termination','Local switching/AP','Routing and security policy','Provider/WAN path'],
 [('Router/switch/AP','Inter-network forwarding, local frame forwarding and wireless access have different functions.'),('Patch panel','Passive termination that supports organized structured cabling.'),('802.3af/at/bt','PoE families with different power capabilities; verify actual device budgets and supported classes.'),('Cable/DSL/fiber/cellular/satellite','Access technologies with different physical and operational constraints.'),('LAN/WLAN/PAN/MAN/WAN/SAN','Local, wireless-local, personal, metropolitan, wide-area and storage-area scopes/purposes; SAN is not simply any file-sharing LAN.'),('Crimper/punchdown/toner/tester/tap','Terminate plugs, terminate IDC connections, trace cables, test characteristics and observe traffic using distinct tools.')],
 'A 24-port PoE switch powers only some newly added phones. Which evidence should be checked?', 'Total and per-port power budget, negotiated device needs and the cable path', ['The number of sockets alone proves sufficient power','The DNS suffix controls available watts'], 'Port count and power budget are separate constraints.',
 'A technician needs to terminate a permanent run onto an IDC block. Which tool category fits?', 'A compatible punchdown tool under the cabling specification', ['A packet capture program as the termination tool','A Wi-Fi analyzer as the termination tool'], 'Physical termination and logical protocol analysis solve different problems.',
 ['Draw a small-office path from client through switch/AP, router/firewall and provider device.','Add a patch panel without assigning it routing behavior.','Compute a hypothetical PoE budget from supplied device ratings.','List three shared dependencies that could defeat a backup internet connection.'],[('IEEE 802.3 Ethernet working group','https://www.ieee802.org/3/')],['core1-2.7','core1-2.8'])

lesson('core1-3.1','display-technology','Display panels, signal paths and image attributes',
 'A display fault can arise in the panel, backlight, digitizer, signal source, cable, adapter or selected mode. An LCD modulates a backlight; IPS, TN and VA describe panel technologies with different viewing, response and contrast tradeoffs. Mini-LED usually describes a backlight approach for an LCD, whereas OLED uses emissive pixels and does not require the same backlight arrangement. An inverter is associated with certain older fluorescent-backlit designs, not every modern panel. A touch digitizer detects input separately from generating the image, so a clear picture with failed touch is a different boundary from a black display. Resolution describes pixel dimensions; pixel density also depends on physical size. Refresh rate is updates per second, not the same as resolution or achieved game frame rate. Color gamut describes the range of reproducible colors; it does not alone prove calibration accuracy. Verify the entire GPU/port/cable/adapter/display path for the intended resolution, refresh and feature set.',
 ['Application/GPU output','Port and supported signal mode','Cable/adapter path','Panel, light and touch components'],
 [('LCD IPS/TN/VA','Different liquid-crystal panel approaches; compare use-case tradeoffs rather than assigning one universal winner.'),('OLED','Emissive pixels; image-retention/burn-in concerns differ from LCD backlight faults.'),('Mini-LED','A fine-grained backlight approach, commonly paired with LCD technology.'),('Digitizer','Touch/input sensing component; can fail separately from the visible image.'),('Resolution/density/refresh/gamut','Pixel dimensions, pixels per physical unit, refresh frequency and color range measure different attributes.')],
 'A tablet displays a sharp image but touch input fails in every application. What component boundary is implicated?', 'The digitizer/input path and its configuration, not automatically the image panel itself', ['Only the internet gateway','A functioning image proves every touch component is healthy'], 'Image production and touch sensing are separate functions that can fail independently.',
 'A monitor supports a high-refresh mode but an adapter limits output to a lower mode. What should the technician conclude?', 'The supported end-to-end signal path constrains the usable mode', ['The monitor label overrides every cable and adapter limit','Increasing screen size changes the protocol bandwidth'], 'Every relevant component in the path must support the requested signal configuration.',
 ['Label image, backlight and touch paths on a diagram.','Build a mode-capability matrix for host, cable, adapter and display.','Distinguish a wrong input from a backlight failure and unsupported mode.','Record a calibration/verification step appropriate to the use case.'],[MS],['core1-5.3'])

lesson('core1-3.2','cable-connector-reference','Cable media, connector identification and installation constraints',
 'Connector recognition is only the first step in selecting a cable. Verify the electrical or optical interface, bandwidth, distance, environmental rating and device requirements. Twisted-pair category ratings describe supported performance under specified installation conditions; poor termination or excessive length can invalidate the intended link. T568A and T568B are pair-to-pin schemes: a consistently terminated straight-through run uses the same scheme at both ends. A plenum rating concerns fire/smoke suitability for specified air-handling spaces, not higher network speed. Shielding and direct-burial ratings solve different environmental requirements. Fiber selection includes single-mode or multimode media, optic wavelength, reach and connector/polish compatibility; a fitting connector alone is insufficient. Video adapters may be active or passive and directional. Legacy connectors remain relevant when supporting installed equipment, but should not be mistaken for evidence of current performance or security features.',
 ['Physical connector','Protocol and media type','Distance/performance specification','Environmental and installation rating'],
 [('RJ11/RJ45','Common telephone-style versus Ethernet twisted-pair connector families; identify the actual pinout and use.'),('F-type','Threaded coaxial connector commonly associated with cable/RF systems.'),('ST/SC/LC','Fiber connector families with different mechanical forms; media and optics still must match.'),('DB9','Legacy serial-style connector family; the physical shell alone does not specify every signal pinout.'),('Molex/SATA power','Distinct power connection families; do not force a connector or assume adapter quality.'),('HDMI/DisplayPort/DVI/VGA','Digital audio/video, display-interface, legacy digital/mixed variants and analog video families respectively.'),('SATA/eSATA','Internal and external serial storage interfaces; data signaling and power delivery must be considered separately.'),('USB 2.0/USB 3.x','Protocol families with different throughput capabilities; versions and lanes matter independently of connector shape.'),('Single-mode/multimode','Different fiber propagation designs paired with suitable optics.'),('Plenum/shielded/direct-burial','Fire/smoke, interference and outdoor-environment constraints are separate ratings.')],
 'A cable is advertised as plenum-rated. What does that establish by itself?', 'A relevant installation/fire-smoke characteristic, not a guarantee of faster data transfer', ['It must be faster than every non-plenum cable','It automatically supports every fiber wavelength'], 'Environmental rating and signal-performance rating answer different selection questions.',
 'Two matching LC connectors are joined, but the optical link fails. What must still be checked?', 'Fiber type, wavelength, optic compatibility, polarity, cleanliness and loss budget', ['Connector shape proves all optical properties match','A DNS change repairs every optical mismatch'], 'Mechanical fit does not establish optical compatibility or a viable signal path.',
 ['Create connector identification cards using vendor images you are permitted to use.','Label straight-through pinout consistency and explain why split pairs matter.','Choose media and jacket requirements for indoor and outdoor examples.','Trace one video adapter’s supported direction and active/passive requirement.'],[('IEEE Ethernet standards information','https://www.ieee802.org/3/')],['core1-2.8'])

lesson('core1-3.3','memory-compatibility','DDR generations, form factors, ECC and channels',
 'A memory purchase must match the processor, board, firmware and physical form factor. DDR generations use different electrical and mechanical standards and are not freely interchangeable. DIMM commonly refers to desktop/server modules; SO-DIMM is a smaller form used by many portable systems, while some devices have soldered memory instead. ECC capability requires a compatible platform and supported module type; ECC, registered/buffered design and capacity are related selection dimensions, not synonyms. Multiple memory channels can improve bandwidth when supported modules are populated according to the vendor’s rules. Two modules installed in adjacent slots do not necessarily mean the desired channel configuration is active. Capacity, supported speeds, timings, rank/population constraints and firmware support can affect stability. A module’s advertised speed is not proof the whole platform will run it at that setting. Diagnose failures with power isolated, seating and compatibility checks, vendor POST indicators and appropriate memory tests; do not assume every crash is caused by RAM.',
 ['CPU memory controller','Board/firmware support','Compatible modules and population','Tested operating configuration'],
 [('DDR generation','Match the supported generation; physical notches and electrical requirements differ.'),('DIMM/SO-DIMM','Physical form factors; device serviceability and exact platform support still matter.'),('ECC/non-ECC','Error-correction capability versus its absence; platform support is required.'),('Memory channel','Independent data path from controller to memory; population rules determine usable arrangements.'),('Supported versus advertised speed','The negotiated stable configuration can differ from a module’s marketing specification.')],
 'A technician installs two identical modules but bandwidth remains below the expected dual-channel arrangement. What should be checked?', 'The board’s recommended slot population and the controller’s reported channel mode', ['Assume any two occupied slots guarantee the desired channel layout','Reformat the system drive'], 'Channel topology is defined by the platform; correct modules in the wrong population pattern may not use the intended arrangement.',
 'ECC memory physically fits a consumer board. What is the appropriate compatibility decision?', 'Check explicit CPU, board and firmware support for that module type before use', ['Assume physical fit guarantees active error correction','Assume ECC and registered memory always mean the same thing'], 'ECC support and module buffering/registration are distinct capabilities that must match the platform.',
 ['Build a hypothetical compatibility matrix for CPU, board, generation, form factor and capacity.','Mark the recommended two-module slots from a vendor manual.','State how you would verify channel mode and stability.','Explain why a marketing speed alone is insufficient evidence.'],[MEM],['core1-5.1'])

lesson('core1-3.4','storage-and-raid','Storage interfaces, RAID calculations and recovery boundaries',
 'Separate storage media, form factor and interface. A rotating HDD has mechanical latency and may be described by spindle speed and a 2.5-inch or 3.5-inch form factor. SSDs remove rotating media but still differ in controller, interface, workload suitability and endurance. NVMe commonly uses PCIe; SATA uses a different protocol path. M.2 and mSATA are form factors/interfaces that require exact platform support, and a physically fitting module is not proof of protocol compatibility. SAS is a storage interface used in compatible systems. RAID changes performance and fault-tolerance properties across disks, but does not protect against every controller failure, deletion, malware event or site loss. Calculate usable capacity from the level and smallest member size, then separately consider rebuild exposure and performance. Always verify the actual implementation’s supported failure combinations and procedures rather than assuming a simple drive-count rule covers every array state.',
 ['Media/form factor','Protocol/controller path','Array layout and fault tolerance','Independent recovery copy'],
 [('RAID 0','Striping; usable capacity is normally the sum of equal-sized members, with no redundancy.'),('RAID 1','Mirroring; a basic two-disk mirror normally provides one member’s usable capacity and tolerates one member failure.'),('RAID 5','Distributed single parity; normally (N−1) × smallest member size, with at least three disks and one-disk failure tolerance.'),('RAID 6','Dual parity; normally (N−2) × smallest member size, with at least four disks and two-disk failure tolerance.'),('RAID 10','Stripe of mirrored pairs; normally half of equal-sized raw capacity, requiring at least four disks. Surviving multiple failures depends on which mirrors lose members.'),('NVMe/SATA/SAS','Different interface/protocol families; check the full controller and device path.'),('Flash cards/USB/optical','Removable media differ in endurance, interface and supported use; no medium is automatically a verified backup.')],
 'Four equal 2 TB disks form a conventional RAID 5 array. Ignoring formatting overhead, what usable capacity is expected?', '6 TB', ['8 TB with no parity cost','4 TB because every RAID level mirrors half the disks'], 'Single parity consumes the equivalent of one member: (4−1) × 2 TB = 6 TB.',
 'A RAID 10 array loses two disks. Can it always survive?', 'No; survival depends on whether both failed disks belong to the same mirrored pair and the implementation state', ['Yes, any two failures are always harmless','No RAID 10 can ever survive two failures'], 'Drive count alone does not describe the failed mirrors. RAID topology and current state determine survivability.',
 ['Draw RAID 0, 1, 5, 6 and 10 using colored blocks on paper.','Calculate usable capacity for equal and unequal member examples.','Mark tolerated failures and a counterexample for RAID 10.','Explain a deletion or site-loss event that RAID does not solve.'],[('SNIA storage education','https://www.snia.org/education')],['core1-5.2'])

lesson('core1-3.5','platform-boot-trust','Motherboards, firmware, CPU architecture and trusted boot',
 'Platform compatibility is a chain: chassis and board form factor, power connections, CPU socket and supported processor list, firmware revision, memory and expansion capabilities, and thermal design all matter. ATX, microATX and ITX describe board-format families, not universal feature guarantees. PCIe links have lane and generation constraints; a connector’s physical length does not guarantee that every lane is electrically available. Firmware boot order and the chosen boot mode must match the installed system. UEFI is a modern firmware interface; Secure Boot checks authorized boot components under its trust configuration, while a TPM can protect keys and record measurements. Neither feature is a replacement for updates or backups. A firmware setup password and an OS account password protect different boundaries. x86/x64 and ARM are architecture families with software compatibility implications; more cores do not automatically accelerate a single-threaded workload. Virtualization support must be present, enabled where required and compatible with the selected hypervisor.',
 ['Chassis/board/power fit','CPU/firmware/memory compatibility','Boot trust and key protection','OS and workload compatibility'],
 [('ATX/microATX/ITX','Board form-factor families affecting fit and expansion; consult the exact model.'),('PCI/PCIe/M.2/headers','Distinct expansion and connection mechanisms with model-specific lane and protocol limits.'),('UEFI boot order','Firmware selection of supported boot paths; wrong order can resemble a missing OS.'),('Secure Boot','Authorized boot-component verification under a configured trust policy.'),('TPM/HSM','Hardware-backed cryptographic capabilities with different form factors and deployment purposes.'),('x86/x64/ARM','Architecture families; application, driver and OS compatibility must be verified.'),('Heat sink/paste/pad/fan/liquid cooling','Different heat-transfer and removal elements; assembly and maintenance are model-specific.')],
 'A new CPU has the right socket but is absent from the board’s supported list until a later firmware revision. What is the relevant check?', 'The board’s supported CPU/firmware combination and approved update path', ['Socket shape alone overrides firmware requirements','Changing DNS adds CPU support'], 'Mechanical compatibility is necessary but may not be sufficient for initialization support.',
 'A user assumes enabling Secure Boot creates an independent backup. What is the correction?', 'Boot trust validation and backup/recovery are separate controls', ['Secure Boot restores deleted documents automatically','A TPM makes every boot component trusted regardless of configuration'], 'Trusted-boot and key-protection mechanisms do not supply data recovery by themselves.',
 ['Draw the dependency chain for a proposed CPU/board upgrade.','Distinguish firmware password, Secure Boot, TPM and OS authentication.','List checks for expansion lanes and thermal/power budget.','Identify recovery keys and backup requirements before firmware or boot-mode changes.'],[MS],['core1-5.1','core2-1.3'])

lesson('core1-3.6','power-budget-safety','PSU rails, connectors, modular cables and power budgets',
 'A power supply converts a supported AC input into DC outputs used by the system. Verify the exact input range and automatic/manual selection behavior from its label and instructions; never assume a unit is safe on every mains system. Common PC rails include 3.3 V, 5 V and 12 V, but connector pinout and device requirements determine which supply reaches each load. A 20+4-pin motherboard connector differs from CPU/EPS and graphics power connections. Similar-looking connectors must not be forced or treated as interchangeable. Modular PSU-side pinouts are not universally standardized between models; use only explicitly compatible cables. Wattage rating indicates capacity under specified conditions, not constant consumption. Efficiency describes the relationship between input and useful output, not a guarantee of build quality, sufficient capacity or zero heat. Redundant supplies improve availability only when configured with appropriate capacity and independent upstream paths; both plugged into one failed circuit can share the same outage.',
 ['Supported AC input','PSU output/rail capacity','Correct connector/pinout','System load and thermal margin'],
 [('3.3/5/12 V outputs','DC rails with specific connector and load requirements.'),('20+4 motherboard power','Common main-board power connector family; separate CPU/GPU connectors have their own requirements.'),('Modular cable','PSU-side compatibility must be explicitly established for the exact model.'),('Wattage versus consumption','Capacity rating differs from actual workload draw.'),('Efficiency versus redundancy','Conversion loss and service continuity are different properties.')],
 'A replacement modular PSU accepts an old cable mechanically. Is that enough to reuse it?', 'No; exact PSU-side compatibility must be documented', ['Yes, matching plugs guarantee identical pin assignments','Yes, equal wattage guarantees identical pin assignments'], 'Incorrect modular pinouts can damage components even when the connector fits.',
 'Two redundant PSUs are both connected to one power strip on one circuit. What risk remains?', 'The shared upstream supply can interrupt both units', ['Redundancy eliminates every power dependency','Efficiency rating guarantees the circuit cannot fail'], 'Redundancy analysis must include upstream and downstream shared failure points.',
 ['Make a power-budget worksheet from hypothetical component ratings.','Separate capacity, efficiency and measured consumption.','Draw independent and shared upstream power paths.','Write the safe replacement steps without opening a PSU enclosure.'],[('Intel ATX power design reference','https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/content-details/336521/atx-version-3-0-multi-rail-desktop-platform-power-supply-design-guide.html')])

lesson('core1-3.7','printer-pipeline','Print and scan pipelines, languages, queues and privacy',
 'Treat printing as a pipeline: an application renders a job, a driver produces a supported format, a queue/spooler schedules it, a connection carries it and the device interprets and marks the page. PCL and PostScript are printer-language families; a model may support one, both or a device-specific driver, so selecting a superficially similar driver can produce garbled output or missing features. A device-generated test page bypasses much of the client path and is a useful isolation boundary. Duplex, orientation, tray selection and quality are job/device settings rather than interchangeable fault fixes. Shared printing may use a server or direct client connections, each with identity, permissions and network dependencies. Secure release, badging and audit logs reduce exposure but do not replace authorization and retention policy. Scanning introduces a different outbound workflow: email, SMB or cloud delivery depends on destination permissions, supported authentication, naming, quotas and connectivity. An ADF feeds multiple pages; a flatbed handles supported individual items with different physical constraints.',
 ['Application/driver language','Queue/spooler/permission','Connection and device interpreter','Print engine or scan destination'],
 [('PCL/PostScript','Printer-language families; use a supported driver for the exact device and OS.'),('Spooler/queue','Job scheduling and state; a stuck job is not automatically a failed print engine.'),('Duplex/tray/orientation/quality','Separate output settings that must match job and media requirements.'),('Secure release/badging/logs','Identity and audit controls for shared-device workflows.'),('ADF/flatbed','Different page-feeding and scanning mechanisms.'),('Scan to email/SMB/cloud','Outbound delivery with its own identity, path and destination permissions.')],
 'A printer’s internal test page is clear, but one PC prints random characters. What is a useful next check?', 'The PC’s selected driver/language and generated job path', ['Replace the fuser first without client checks','Assume a valid test page proves every client driver is correct'], 'The internal page verifies a different part of the pipeline than the workstation job.',
 'Printing works but scan-to-SMB fails after a service account password change. What should be checked?', 'The scan destination’s configured identity, permissions and authentication error', ['Replace toner to restore SMB authentication','Assume inbound printing proves outbound scanning credentials are valid'], 'Printing and scan delivery use different directions and potentially different credentials.',
 ['Draw a print pipeline and a scan-to-share pipeline separately.','Place the device test page at its isolation boundary.','Choose supported driver and secure-release settings for a fictional office.','Define a post-change test for both printing and scanning.'],[MS],['core1-5.6'])

lesson('core1-3.8','printer-mechanisms','Laser, inkjet, thermal and impact maintenance',
 'Identify the technology and symptom before choosing a consumable or maintenance action. A laser/LED-style electrophotographic engine uses charging, exposure, development, transfer, fusing and cleaning stages; vendor designs differ in which parts are integrated. Toner that rubs off can implicate fusing or media/settings, while repeated marks at a consistent interval can suggest a rotating component. An inkjet deposits liquid ink through a printhead; missing lines can result from clogged nozzles, depleted ink or related maintenance needs. Cleaning and calibration should follow the device procedure rather than repeated unrestricted cleaning cycles. Direct thermal printing uses heat-sensitive media; loading the wrong side or wrong paper can produce blank output. Thermal-transfer devices use a ribbon, so do not generalize every thermal design as ribbonless. Impact printing strikes a ribbon and can support suitable multipart forms. Feed rollers, separation mechanisms, media condition and obstruction can affect multiple technologies. Hot, moving and high-voltage components require proper service precautions.',
 ['Identify technology','Classify image versus feed symptom','Use supported maintenance','Verify with suitable test output'],
 [('Laser stages','Charge → expose → develop → transfer → fuse → clean, with vendor-specific implementation.'),('Inkjet nozzle/printhead','Deposits ink; use supported cleaning, alignment and consumable checks.'),('Direct thermal','Uses heat-sensitive media, whose type and orientation matter.'),('Thermal transfer','Uses an appropriate ribbon to transfer image material.'),('Impact/multipart forms','Mechanical strikes through a ribbon onto supported forms.'),('Feed/roller/separation','Paper handling faults can be distinct from the image-forming process.')],
 'A direct-thermal printer feeds correctly but produces blank labels after a media change. What should be checked first?', 'Correct heat-sensitive media and its loading orientation', ['Install laser toner','Replace the Ethernet cable as the only possible cause'], 'A functioning feed path does not establish that the correct imaging surface is exposed to heat.',
 'Laser toner rubs off a printed page. Which area warrants investigation under the service procedure?', 'Fusing, compatible media and related print settings', ['Only the DNS resolver','Only the keyboard driver'], 'Toner adhesion is related to the thermal/pressure process and media compatibility, not name resolution.',
 ['Annotate a laser image-forming diagram without servicing live high-voltage parts.','Match sample symptoms to imaging, consumable and feed boundaries.','Compare direct thermal with thermal transfer.','Specify a safe test page after maintenance.'],[MS],['core1-5.6'])

lesson('core1-4.1','virtualization-isolation','Hypervisors, containers, VDI and isolation limits',
 'A type-1 hypervisor runs directly on the host platform, while a type-2 hypervisor runs through a host operating system. Product architectures can blur simplified labels, so use the documented design rather than a screenshot alone. Virtual machines provide virtual hardware and run guest operating systems; containers commonly share a host kernel and isolate processes through OS mechanisms. A container is not a complete guest OS with the same isolation assumptions as a VM. VDI delivers managed desktops to clients and introduces broker, identity, display-protocol, network and host-capacity dependencies. Legacy or cross-platform workloads still require architecture, driver and application compatibility checks; virtualization is not universal instruction-set translation. A VM snapshot captures a point-in-time state but may depend on the original storage and does not replace an independent tested backup. A sandbox’s usefulness depends on its actual isolation, shared folders, clipboard, network mode and host protections. Finite host CPU, RAM, storage throughput and network capacity constrain all guests.',
 ['Physical host resources','Hypervisor or shared-kernel runtime','Guest/container isolation configuration','Application and user-access path'],
 [('Type 1/type 2','Direct platform hypervisor versus hosted hypervisor arrangement.'),('VM/container','Virtual hardware plus guest OS versus commonly shared-kernel process isolation.'),('VDI','Remote delivery of centrally managed desktop environments.'),('Snapshot/backup','Dependent point-in-time state versus an appropriately independent recovery copy.'),('Bridged/NAT/isolated virtual networking','Different connectivity paths; verify the hypervisor’s exact mode and policy.')],
 'A malware-analysis VM has a shared host folder and bridged network access. What should be concluded?', 'It is not safely isolated merely because it is a VM; review and restrict the authorized test design', ['VM status guarantees no host or network exposure','Taking a snapshot blocks all outbound traffic'], 'Virtualization does not automatically remove shared-resource and network paths.',
 'Several guests become slow while host RAM is exhausted and storage paging rises. What is the useful next investigation?', 'Guest allocation, active working sets and host resource pressure', ['Increase every guest allocation without checking host capacity','Change only the remote desktop wallpaper'], 'Guests share finite host resources; more assigned memory can worsen contention when physical capacity is exceeded.',
 ['Draw VM and container isolation boundaries separately.','Build a hypothetical host-capacity budget.','Trace a VDI sign-in and display path.','List sandbox sharing/network settings that require explicit review.'],[MS],['core1-4.2'])

lesson('core2-1.1','filesystem-selection','Filesystems, permissions, limits and platform compatibility',
 'Choose a filesystem from required features and supported platforms, not just the capacity printed on the drive. NTFS supports Windows permissions and features such as metadata journaling and, in supported contexts, EFS. exFAT is useful for many cross-platform removable-media workflows but does not provide the same NTFS access-control and journaling feature set. FAT32 is widely recognized by older devices but has a per-file limit of 2^32−1 bytes, so a single file of 4 GiB or more cannot be stored there. A device may impose additional formatting or capacity restrictions. APFS is used by modern Apple systems, while ext4 and XFS are common Linux filesystem choices. ReFS is a Windows resilience-focused filesystem with workload, edition and feature limitations; it is not a universal drop-in boot-volume replacement. Formatting changes metadata and can destroy access to existing data. Verify backups and the target device before any destructive operation. An OS being able to read a volume does not imply identical write support or preservation of every metadata feature.',
 ['Application file requirements','Platform read/write support','Permission and resilience features','Protected format/migration plan'],
 [('NTFS','Windows filesystem with access controls and metadata journaling; feature support is context-dependent.'),('FAT32','Broad compatibility but a single-file limit below 4 GiB and fewer advanced features.'),('exFAT','Often suitable for large removable files across supported platforms; not the NTFS security feature set.'),('APFS','Apple filesystem family used by supported modern Apple systems.'),('ext4/XFS','Linux filesystem choices with different tools and workload characteristics.'),('ReFS','Windows resilience-focused option with specific supported use cases and limitations.')],
 'A 6 GiB video will not copy to a FAT32 USB drive despite sufficient free space. What explains the failure?', 'The file exceeds FAT32’s per-file size limit', ['The filename must be shorter than four characters','All USB drives reject files larger than 1 GiB'], 'Free capacity and maximum single-file size are different constraints.',
 'A team needs Windows file-level access permissions on a data volume. Which selection is the appropriate starting point?', 'A supported NTFS design with correctly configured permissions', ['Assume exFAT preserves the same NTFS ACL semantics','Assume a filesystem label itself authorizes users'], 'Filesystem capabilities and actual access-control configuration both matter.',
 ['Compare six filesystems by platform, large-file, permission and recovery needs.','Calculate why a specific file fails on FAT32.','Plan a nondestructive migration with verified backups.','Distinguish format, partition and mount operations.'],[('Microsoft filesystem feature comparison','https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/fileio/filesystem-functionality-comparison')])

lesson('core2-1.2','installation-and-partitioning','Boot media, GPT/MBR, upgrades and recovery planning',
 'An installation plan begins with required applications, supported hardware, licensing, drivers, user data and recovery options. A clean install replaces the target OS environment and can erase data depending on chosen operations; an in-place upgrade aims to preserve supported applications and settings but still requires backups and compatibility validation. Image deployment, remote/network installation and zero-touch workflows add image, identity, network and automation dependencies. A recovery partition is not automatically an independent backup of every user file. UEFI boot is normally paired with a GPT-based modern Windows deployment, while legacy arrangements may use MBR; confirm the supported combination for the intended OS. MBR partitioning has conventional disk-size and partition-count constraints, whereas GPT provides a different partitioning design. A firmware mode change can prevent an otherwise intact installation from booting. Bootable USB, optical/ISO, internal recovery, network and internet recovery methods each require platform support and appropriate trusted media. Record encryption recovery material and test the actual return-to-service path before making a major change.',
 ['Requirements and protection','Trusted installation/boot media','Supported firmware/partition layout','Drivers, applications and recovery verification'],
 [('Clean/in-place/repair','Different installation intents and preservation behavior; verify the selected operation.'),('GPT/MBR','Different partition schemes; modern firmware/OS support determines the usable combination.'),('USB/network/image deployment','Boot and provisioning methods with distinct connectivity and automation dependencies.'),('Recovery partition/backup','An on-device recovery environment is not a substitute for independent user-data recovery.'),('Feature update/lifecycle','Compatibility and supported update paths matter across OS versions and editions.')],
 'A technician changes legacy boot mode to UEFI on an existing system and the OS disappears from the boot list. What should be investigated?', 'The installed boot layout, firmware mode and supported conversion/recovery path', ['Assume the user files have definitely been erased','Delete every partition immediately'], 'Boot selection can fail even when stored data remains intact; preserve evidence and use a supported recovery plan.',
 'An in-place upgrade is advertised as preserving applications. Does that remove the need for backups and compatibility tests?', 'No; preservation intent is not a guarantee that every app, driver and data requirement will survive', ['Yes, every supported upgrade is an independent backup','Only the wallpaper needs checking'], 'Plan for failures and verify the specific workload rather than relying on a general promise.',
 ['Design a fictional upgrade checklist with recovery keys and data backups.','Compare clean, repair and in-place choices for three symptoms.','Draw boot firmware → bootloader → OS dependencies.','Specify application and driver verification after deployment.'],[MS],['core2-1.3'])

lesson('core2-1.3','windows-editions-and-lifecycle','Edition features, management and supported lifecycle',
 'Windows edition and version are separate dimensions. Home, Pro, Pro for Workstations and Enterprise are not interchangeable feature sets. Requirements such as joining a traditional Active Directory domain, hosting supported Remote Desktop sessions, Group Policy management and particular encryption features must be checked against the exact edition and version. A client may be able to initiate a remote connection without being able to host the same service. N editions omit certain media-related components; an application can depend on those capabilities. Hardware requirements include architecture, memory, storage and supported firmware/security features, with current Windows 11 support requiring attention to supported processor and TPM/UEFI capabilities. Do not assume an installation that can be forced to start is a supported deployment. Lifecycle dates and support offerings change; check the current product lifecycle, servicing channel, organizational agreement and exception process. A certification objective can discuss an older OS for support comparison even when deploying it without a valid support plan would be inappropriate.',
 ['Required management feature','Exact edition/version','Supported hardware and servicing','Validated application deployment'],
 [('Domain/workgroup','Central organizational identity and policy versus local workgroup arrangements.'),('RDP client/host','Initiating a session and accepting supported remote sessions are separate capabilities.'),('Group Policy Editor','Availability and supported management behavior depend on the edition and environment.'),('BitLocker/device encryption','Related data-protection capabilities with edition, device and management constraints.'),('N editions','Media-component differences can affect application dependencies.'),('Lifecycle/ESU/servicing','Verify current support eligibility rather than assuming all versions receive the same updates.')],
 'A laptop must join the company’s traditional domain and host an approved Remote Desktop workflow. What should procurement verify?', 'The exact Windows edition/version supports both required organizational features', ['Any Windows edition has identical domain and hosting capabilities','The keyboard language determines domain support'], 'Feature requirements must be matched to the product, not inferred from the Windows brand alone.',
 'An older OS still boots and runs a required application. Does that establish a supported, secure production deployment?', 'No; verify current servicing eligibility, application support and the organization’s risk decision', ['Yes, successful boot proves ongoing vendor support','Yes, changing the product name restores updates'], 'Operability, support entitlement and security posture are different facts.',
 ['Build an edition-feature decision table using current Microsoft documentation.','Separate RDP client and host requirements.','Check lifecycle status and record the verification date.','Write a compatibility and migration plan for a legacy application.'],[MS])

lesson('core2-1.4','windows-evidence-tools','Choosing Windows tools from the question being investigated',
 'Start with the symptom and the evidence needed. Task Manager shows processes, resource use, startup entries, services and users at a useful first-pass level. Resource Monitor gives more detail on resource activity, while Performance Monitor supports counters and longer observations. Event Viewer contains event records; correlate provider, event time and context rather than treating every warning as the cause. Device Manager addresses device recognition and driver state. Disk Management concerns disk/volume layout, not every storage-health question. Task Scheduler manages triggered tasks and can explain unexpected recurring work. Certificate Manager inspects relevant certificate stores and trust details. System Information records platform and software configuration; System Configuration is a troubleshooting tool, not a reason to disable services indiscriminately. Registry Editor changes a sensitive configuration database: collect evidence and use documented, reversible procedures rather than deleting keys speculatively. A tool opening successfully does not prove that the target subsystem is healthy.',
 ['Question and time window','Appropriate evidence tool','Correlated observations','Controlled hypothesis and verification'],
 [('taskmgr/resmon/perfmon','Process/resource summary, detailed activity and performance counters respectively.'),('eventvwr.msc','Event records with provider, severity, identifiers, time and context.'),('devmgmt.msc','Device and driver recognition/status.'),('diskmgmt.msc','Disk, partition and volume layout/management.'),('taskschd.msc','Scheduled task definitions, triggers and history where available.'),('certmgr.msc','Certificates in the targeted store/context.'),('lusrmgr.msc/gpedit.msc','Local account/group and policy tools where supported by the edition.'),('msinfo32/msconfig','Configuration inventory and controlled startup troubleshooting.'),('cleanmgr/dfrgui/regedit','Cleanup, supported drive optimization and registry editing are different operations.')],
 'A workstation becomes slow every day at the same time. Which evidence combination is useful?', 'Resource/process observation and scheduled-task/event correlation around that time', ['Delete every warning in Event Viewer','Replace RAM solely because the clock time repeats'], 'A repeatable time window can correlate with scheduled work; evidence should guide a testable hypothesis.',
 'A device works before a driver update and fails immediately afterward. Which tool and recovery boundary fit?', 'Inspect the device/driver state and evaluate a supported targeted rollback', ['Repartition all disks first','Disable every scheduled task'], 'A narrow recent change supports a scoped driver hypothesis before broad system changes.',
 ['Match five symptoms to tools and the evidence expected.','Create a fictional timestamped timeline from process, task and event records.','Distinguish observation from configuration-changing actions.','Define a post-fix test reproducing the original trigger.'],[MS],['core2-3.1'])

lesson('core2-1.5','windows-command-map','Command-line evidence and destructive-operation boundaries',
 'A command should answer a specific question with the least authority and impact needed. Read built-in help and current vendor documentation before using switches. Navigation and listing commands are different from deletion or formatting operations. ipconfig reports address settings; ping tests an ICMP echo path where permitted; tracert and pathping investigate paths and loss with filtering caveats; nslookup queries DNS; netstat reports relevant connection/listener information. A missing ping reply is not proof a host is offline. sfc checks protected system files, whereas chkdsk concerns filesystem/volume integrity and can have repair or availability implications depending on options. DiskPart and format can destroy access to data when used incorrectly, so identifying a disk by current evidence and protecting data are essential. robocopy is a powerful copy tool whose mirroring/deletion options can remove destination content; a dry-run/listing and review are valuable in test workflows. gpresult reports resulting policy, while gpupdate requests policy refresh. These are related but not interchangeable.',
 ['Question and target','Read-only evidence first','Documented scoped action','Result and side-effect verification'],
 [('cd/dir/md/rmdir','Navigate, list, create directory and remove directory; removal switches change risk.'),('ipconfig/ping/netstat/nslookup','Address, reachability, endpoint and resolver evidence.'),('net use/tracert/pathping','Network resource connections and route/loss investigation.'),('chkdsk/sfc','Filesystem checking versus protected system-file checking.'),('format/diskpart','Storage configuration operations that can be destructive; use only an authorized protected plan.'),('robocopy','Structured copy with powerful options; inspect mirroring, deletion, retries and logs.'),('hostname/whoami/net user/winver','Machine name, current identity, user-account information and OS version context.'),('gpupdate/gpresult','Policy refresh versus the resulting applied-policy report.'),('command /?','Built-in usage help; confirm applicable syntax and effects.')],
 'A user is affected by a policy despite a different-looking local setting. Which command helps inspect the applied result?', 'gpresult in the appropriate user/computer context', ['format as the policy reporting tool','gpupdate is identical to a detailed resulting-policy report'], 'Refreshing policy and reporting effective policy answer different questions.',
 'A technician proposes a mirroring copy to a folder containing unique files. What must be checked first?', 'Whether the selected options delete destination-only content, with a protected test and reviewed log/listing', ['Assume every copy option is nondestructive','Judge safety only by the command length'], 'Mirroring can remove destination entries to match the source; protecting unique data comes before execution.',
 ['Classify each command as observational, potentially modifying or destructive for the selected options.','Match a DNS, policy and system-file symptom to a command.','Review a hypothetical copy plan for deletion behavior.','Write expected outputs and stop criteria without running a destructive operation.'],[CMD])

lesson('core2-1.7','windows-network-access','Windows network profiles, shares, identities and effective access',
 'A network path involves both transport and authorization. Check the client’s address, subnet, gateway, DNS and proxy configuration before assuming a permission problem. A public or private network profile can select different firewall and discovery behavior, so the category should match the environment under policy rather than be changed merely to bypass a block. A workgroup uses a different management/identity model from a traditional domain-joined computer. Mapping a drive assigns a local connection to a resource; it does not create the share or grant access. A UNC path identifies a network server/share path and can fail because the name, route, service, identity or permissions are wrong. For remote file access, share permissions and filesystem permissions both affect the result, along with group membership, inheritance and applicable deny rules. Local access does not traverse a share permission boundary in the same way. A VPN can establish a path without authorizing a user to every destination. Metered settings can limit background transfers without meaning the network is physically broken.',
 ['Client configuration/name resolution','Route and firewall profile','Service and identity','Share plus filesystem authorization'],
 [('UNC path','A server/share resource path; resolving a name is separate from accessing the share.'),('Mapped drive','A client connection/drive mapping to an existing network resource.'),('Public/private profile','Network category affecting policy and firewall behavior.'),('Share and NTFS permissions','Remote access is constrained by both applicable permission layers.'),('Proxy/VPN/metered connection','Application routing, virtual connectivity and usage policy solve different requirements.')],
 'A user can open a file locally but receives access denied through a share. What should be reviewed?', 'The remote identity plus both share and filesystem effective permissions', ['Only the filename extension','Assume local success proves share permissions are correct'], 'Remote share access adds a permission and identity context beyond opening a local path.',
 'A VPN connects, but one application is still inaccessible. What is the appropriate next investigation?', 'The destination route, name resolution, service path and application authorization', ['Assume the VPN status grants every application role','Disable all firewalls permanently'], 'A successful tunnel does not prove all downstream network and application requirements.',
 ['Draw local and remote file-access paths separately.','Compute an effective-access example from supplied share and NTFS rules.','Mark where group membership and inheritance enter the decision.','Specify a successful authorized test and a denied unauthorized test.'],[MS],['core2-2.2'])

lesson('core2-1.8','macos-reference','macOS application, security, recovery and navigation tools',
 'Support the actual macOS version and device architecture rather than assuming every Windows procedure has a direct equivalent. Applications may arrive as .app bundles, installer packages or disk images; a .dmg commonly mounts a disk image and is not automatically an installed application. Use approved sources, organizational restrictions and the supported installation/uninstallation process. System and user libraries have different scopes, and editing protected system locations is not a routine substitute for diagnosis. Finder navigates files, Spotlight searches indexed content, Mission Control organizes windows/desktops, and Force Quit terminates a nonresponsive application without promising unsaved data recovery. Keychain manages credentials and trust-related items; FileVault protects supported startup-volume data at rest. Time Machine is a backup workflow that requires a configured destination and usable history, not a guarantee that every file is independently recoverable. Disk Utility examines and manages supported storage structures; erase and partition actions need data protection. Continuity and iCloud features depend on account, device, network and management settings.',
 ['Trusted app/source','User versus system scope','Identity/data protection','Verified backup and recovery'],
 [('.dmg/.pkg/.app','Disk image, installer package and application bundle serve different roles.'),('/Applications and /Users','Typical system application and user-home scopes.'),('/Library, user Library, /System','Different configuration/support and protected-system scopes.'),('Finder/Spotlight/Mission Control','Navigation, indexed search and window/desktop organization.'),('Keychain/FileVault','Credential/trust storage versus supported disk encryption.'),('Time Machine/Disk Utility','Backup history versus storage inspection/management.'),('Continuity/iCloud','Cross-device/cloud features whose account and policy dependencies must be checked.'),('Terminal/Force Quit','Command environment and application termination; neither implies a safe repair automatically.')],
 'A downloaded .dmg opens a mounted image, but the app disappears after it is ejected. What should be checked?', 'Whether the application was installed according to its approved instructions instead of only launched from the image', ['Assume every mounted image permanently installs all its contents','Erase the startup disk'], 'Mounting installation media and installing an application are separate steps.',
 'A Mac is encrypted with FileVault. Does that prove a deleted file can be restored?', 'No; encryption and a usable backup/version history are separate requirements', ['Yes, encryption creates an independent historical copy','Yes, Keychain stores all deleted files'], 'Confidentiality protection does not establish recoverability.',
 ['Map each macOS tool to a concrete support question.','Distinguish package/image/bundle deployment.','Draw user and system configuration scopes.','Describe a restoration test using a fictional Time Machine history.'],[APPLE])

lesson('core2-1.9','linux-reference','Linux commands, permissions and configuration boundaries',
 'Linux support requires understanding the distribution, permissions and service context. The kernel manages core OS functions; a bootloader starts a selected system path, and systemd is a common service/init manager on many distributions rather than every Linux system. Paths are hierarchical and case-sensitive in common filesystems. Read manual pages and package documentation for the actual installed tools. ls and pwd report location/content; cp and mv copy/move, while rm removes entries and can be destructive. chmod changes mode permissions and chown changes ownership; neither should be used as a blanket make-everything-writable fix. sudo grants configured command authority, while su switches identity under its authentication rules. Package managers such as apt and dnf differ by distribution and repository policy. ip reports/configures network state, ping tests permitted echo reachability, curl makes supported URL requests and dig inspects DNS. Filesystem checks and mounts require careful targeting and supported state; do not run repair commands against arbitrary live storage. Configuration files also have different authority and sensitivity.',
 ['Distribution and current identity','File/service/network evidence','Scoped authorized change','Verification and rollback'],
 [('ls/pwd/cat/grep/find','List, locate current directory, read content, search text and find path entries.'),('cp/mv/rm','Copy, move and remove; verify targets and options before changes.'),('chmod/chown/sudo/su','Mode, ownership, configured privilege execution and identity switching.'),('apt/dnf','Distribution-specific package management with trusted repositories and dependencies.'),('ip/ping/curl/dig/traceroute','Different network configuration, reachability, application and path investigations.'),('ps/top/du/df','Process state/activity, directory usage and filesystem capacity perspectives.'),('/etc/passwd and /etc/shadow','Account metadata versus restricted password-related data; do not expose sensitive files.'),('/etc/hosts, fstab, resolv.conf','Static name mappings, filesystem mount configuration and resolver-related configuration; management mechanisms may generate files.'),('mount/fsck/man/nano','Filesystem attachment/checking, usage documentation and a text editor.')],
 'A Linux web service cannot read one required directory. What is the correct permission investigation?', 'Identify the service account and necessary path permissions, then grant only the required access', ['Set every directory to world-writable','Run all services as root permanently'], 'Effective access depends on identity, ownership and permissions along the path; a blanket relaxation creates unrelated risk.',
 'A user reports disk-full errors, but a directory-size estimate and filesystem free-space report differ. What should support understand?', 'du and df measure different perspectives, and open-deleted files, mounts or allocation details can matter', ['The commands must always produce identical numbers','A mismatch proves the keyboard is faulty'], 'Used directory entries and filesystem allocation are related but not identical views; collect context before deleting data.',
 ['Create a fictional file-permission matrix and compute access for a service account.','Match process, capacity and DNS symptoms to commands.','Explain the sensitivity of shadow versus public account metadata.','Plan a reversible configuration change using distribution documentation.'],[LINUX])

lesson('core2-2.2','windows-security-boundaries','Accounts, UAC, encryption and directory policy',
 'Authentication verifies an identity; authorization evaluates what that identity may do. A standard account and an administrator have different authority, and choosing Run as administrator can change the security context for a specific process. UAC is an elevation mechanism, not a substitute for least privilege or proof an executable is trustworthy. Local and Microsoft-connected accounts differ from traditional domain identities and centrally managed policy. Windows Hello and passwordless methods rely on supported device/identity arrangements; a PIN is not simply a reusable domain password. File access can depend on ownership, group membership, inherited permissions and explicit rules. Remote SMB access also encounters share permissions. BitLocker protects supported volumes, BitLocker To Go applies to supported removable-drive scenarios, and EFS protects selected files with certificate/key dependencies; losing recovery material can make data inaccessible. Group Policy, organizational units, security groups, home folders, logon scripts and folder redirection are distinct management mechanisms with different scopes and effects.',
 ['Identity and authentication','Process authority/elevation','Resource access rules','Encryption and recovery material'],
 [('Standard/admin/guest contexts','Different authority and intended use; do not share broad privilege for convenience.'),('UAC and Run as administrator','Elevation/context mechanisms rather than an executable trust guarantee.'),('NTFS/share/inheritance','Applicable resource rules combine with identity and group membership.'),('BitLocker/To Go/EFS','Volume/removable-volume/file protection with different keys and supported configurations.'),('AD groups/OUs/Group Policy','Access membership, organization and configuration policy are separate concepts.'),('Home folder/folder redirection/logon script','Different ways to provide resources or run supported sign-in actions.')],
 'An EFS-protected file is copied to a new user profile without its decryption certificate/key. What risk must be addressed?', 'The new context may be unable to decrypt it; follow the approved key/recovery process', ['Renaming the file removes encryption','Administrator status always makes every missing decryption key unnecessary'], 'Authorization and possession of appropriate decryption material are not interchangeable.',
 'A user approves a UAC prompt for an unknown program. What does that approval establish?', 'Permission to elevate in that context, not proof the program is safe', ['The program passed a complete security audit','The program cannot modify sensitive data'], 'A privilege prompt controls authority; it does not independently validate software behavior.',
 ['Draw authentication, authorization, elevation and decryption as separate gates.','Compute an access example with groups, inheritance and share rules.','Create a recovery-material checklist using no real secrets.','Compare a group assignment with a Group Policy configuration.'],[MS],['core2-2.7'])

lesson('core2-2.4','malware-response','Malware behaviors, detection and controlled recovery',
 'Classify observable behavior without treating a label as complete attribution. A virus attaches to suitable host content, a worm can propagate, a Trojan misrepresents its purpose, spyware and stalkerware monitor activity, and a keylogger records input. Rootkits can conceal or alter system visibility; boot-sector and fileless techniques target different execution/persistence paths. Ransomware can both disrupt access and steal data, while a cryptominer consumes resources for mining. Adware and potentially unwanted programs may be intrusive without every instance meeting the same malware definition. Antivirus, anti-malware, EDR, MDR and XDR describe different detection, telemetry and managed-response capabilities rather than interchangeable product guarantees. Follow the organization’s incident process: verify symptoms, contain as appropriate, preserve evidence, eradicate or rebuild, restore safely, update controls and verify behavior afterward. A simplified exam cleanup sequence is not blanket authority to delete logs, disable recovery or destroy evidence in a real incident. Recurrent symptoms warrant investigation of persistence, entry point and affected identities.',
 ['Verify and preserve evidence','Contain authorized scope','Eradicate or rebuild safely','Recover, verify and improve'],
 [('Virus/worm/Trojan','Attachment, propagation and deceptive delivery concepts can overlap in real campaigns.'),('Spyware/keylogger/stalkerware','Monitoring behaviors with privacy and abuse implications.'),('Rootkit/boot/fileless','Concealment and execution/persistence mechanisms needing appropriate investigation.'),('Ransomware/cryptominer','Extortion/disruption/data theft versus resource exploitation patterns.'),('AV/EDR/MDR/XDR','Malware detection, endpoint telemetry/response, managed response and cross-source capabilities vary by implementation.'),('Reimage/cleanup/recovery','Choose a trusted restoration path according to compromise, evidence and policy requirements.')],
 'An endpoint appears clean after a scan, but the same unauthorized process returns at reboot. What remains to investigate?', 'Persistence, the entry path and whether a trusted rebuild is required', ['Only close the notification permanently','Assume the clean scan proves the incident is resolved'], 'One detection result does not prove removal of the underlying cause or a trustworthy recovered system.',
 'A basic cleanup checklist says to disable a recovery feature. May a technician apply that to every corporate incident without review?', 'No; follow the authorized response and evidence-preservation process for the actual environment', ['Yes, an exam-style sequence overrides organizational authority','Yes, deleting all historical data always improves forensics'], 'Study workflows are not universal permission to change recovery or evidence controls.',
 ['Classify fictional symptoms by plausible behaviors without claiming certain attribution.','Build a containment/eradication/recovery decision record.','Identify evidence and data that must be preserved.','Define post-recovery tests and a user-education follow-up.'],[MS],['core2-2.6','core2-3.3','core2-3.4'])

lesson('core2-2.9','sanitization-and-media','Sanitization choices, verification and disposal evidence',
 'Deleting a directory entry, quick-formatting a volume and sanitizing media are different operations. The correct method depends on technology, data sensitivity, intended reuse, organizational requirements and applicable obligations. Magnetic HDD and solid-state storage do not share identical remanence and controller behavior. Wear leveling, spare areas and inaccessible blocks can make simplistic overwrite assumptions unreliable for some flash devices. Cryptographic erase relies on appropriate prior encryption and effective destruction of the relevant keys; merely removing a user password is not equivalent. Degaussing applies to suitable magnetic media, not as a universal flash-sanitization method. Physical destruction must meet the approved standard and must not create unsafe handling or environmental harm. Outsourcing does not remove accountability: identify media, maintain custody records, define the required outcome, obtain appropriate evidence and verify the vendor process. A certificate by itself should be checked against the actual assets and scope. Real sanitization is destructive and requires explicit authorization and verified targets.',
 ['Identify media and data obligations','Choose approved clear/purge/destroy outcome','Authorize and verify targets','Record custody and verified disposition'],
 [('Delete/format','Filesystem operations do not automatically establish sanitization.'),('Clear/purge/destroy','Different outcome categories whose suitable methods depend on media and requirements.'),('Degaussing','Magnetic-media technique, not a universal SSD erasure method.'),('Cryptographic erase','Key-destruction approach with prerequisites about encryption and key handling.'),('Third-party destruction record','Evidence must identify actual assets, process and authorized disposition.'),('Recycling/repurposing','Data protection and environmental handling both need a documented process.')],
 'A technician wants to degauss an SSD because that worked on magnetic disks. What is the correct response?', 'Use a supported sanitization method appropriate to solid-state media and the required outcome', ['Degaussing reliably clears every flash cell','Deleting a desktop shortcut is equivalent'], 'Different storage technologies require different sanitization assumptions.',
 'A disposal vendor sends a generic certificate without device identifiers. What should be verified?', 'That the evidence actually covers the organization’s specific assets and required process', ['Assume every certificate covers every device forever','Discard the custody record immediately'], 'Accountability requires a link between the asset, authorized method and verified disposition.',
 ['Write a media-disposition decision tree for HDD, SSD and removable flash.','Include authorization, custody, target verification and evidence.','Explain why quick format is not a general purge guarantee.','Create a fictional vendor handoff record with no real device secrets.'],[('NIST media sanitization guidance','https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/88/r2/final')],['core2-4.6'])

lesson('core2-4.3','backup-chain-worked','Backup chains, RPO/RTO and proving restoration',
 'A backup strategy begins with what must be recovered, how much data loss is acceptable, how quickly service must return and which dependencies restoration requires. A full backup captures the selected dataset. An incremental normally captures changes since the preceding relevant backup and can require a complete chain. A differential normally captures changes since the last full, so a conventional restore uses that full plus the selected differential. Product terminology and application consistency must be checked in the actual system. A scheduled job reporting success proves less than a restoration test that checks the required files, application state, permissions and usable result. RPO concerns tolerated data loss measured in time, while RTO concerns the target duration to restore service. Copies on the same failed storage or accessible to the same destructive identity may share a failure mode. Encryption keys, catalogs, credentials, network access and recovery media are dependencies that can be forgotten until an outage. Test both technical restoration and the documented operational process.',
 ['Recovery requirements','Backup schedule and dependency chain','Independent protected copies','Timed restoration and verification'],
 [('Full/incremental/differential','Whole selected dataset, changes since prior relevant backup and changes since full respectively, subject to product semantics.'),('RPO/RTO','Acceptable data-loss window versus restoration-duration target.'),('File/image/application backup','Different recovery granularity and consistency needs.'),('Online/offline/immutable copies','Different accessibility and tamper-resistance properties; verify actual controls.'),('Restore test','Demonstrate required data and service behavior, not just job completion.')],
 'A conventional plan takes a full backup Sunday and daily incrementals through Thursday. What is generally required to restore Thursday’s state?', 'The usable Sunday full and every required incremental in the chain through Thursday', ['Thursday’s incremental alone is always sufficient','Any old full plus an unrelated differential'], 'Incremental dependencies matter; a missing required component can invalidate later restore points.',
 'The business allows fifteen minutes of data loss and two hours of restoration time. Which mapping is correct?', 'RPO 15 minutes; RTO 2 hours', ['RTO 15 minutes; RPO 2 hours','Both values describe only storage capacity'], 'The loss window and restoration duration are different recovery objectives.',
 ['Draw full-plus-incremental and full-plus-differential restore chains.','Mark what happens when one required file is lost.','Create a restoration test checklist with timing and application verification.','List keys, catalogs, media and identities required for recovery.'],[MS],['core2-4.2'])

lesson('core2-4.8','scripting-and-ai','Automation logic, permission scope and verified AI assistance',
 'Read an automation script as a sequence of inputs, decisions, repeated actions and outputs. Variables hold values; conditionals select a path; loops repeat actions. Batch files, PowerShell, shell scripts, JavaScript and Python have different runtimes, syntax and authority contexts. A small script can affect many machines, so length does not establish safety. Understand target selection, path handling, credentials, error handling, logs and rollback behavior before execution. A service restart may interrupt users; a reimaging or deletion loop can destroy data at scale. Test with nonproduction inputs and the least required permissions. AI assistance can suggest commands and explain errors, but it can hallucinate syntax, infer the wrong environment or expose sensitive input to an external service. Public and private deployments have different data-handling arrangements that must be verified, not assumed from a product label. Review output against trusted documentation and observed state. Automation does not turn an unsupported guess into evidence, and an AI-generated plan still requires authorization for real side effects.',
 ['Inputs and selected targets','Conditional/loop logic','Scoped authority and side effects','Evidence, errors and rollback'],
 [('Variable/conditional/loop','Data storage, branching and repetition building blocks.'),('Batch/PowerShell/shell/JS/Python','Different execution environments and syntax; identify the actual interpreter.'),('Dry run/idempotency','Preview intended work where supported and design repeat execution deliberately.'),('Logs and error handling','Detect partial success, failures and recovery needs.'),('AI hallucination/bias/privacy','Generated content may be wrong or inappropriate; protect sensitive data and verify claims.')],
 'A script selects computers by a broad name pattern before reimaging them. What should be reviewed before execution?', 'The exact target set, authorization, data protection, failure behavior and recovery plan in a test context', ['Only whether the script has fewer than ten lines','Assume a familiar language makes every operation safe'], 'Targeting and side effects determine risk; compact code can have a large blast radius.',
 'An AI tool recommends a command with an unfamiliar destructive switch. What is the appropriate support action?', 'Verify the documented syntax and effects, test safely and obtain the required authorization before real use', ['Run it because the explanation sounds confident','Upload all credentials to improve the answer'], 'Confidence is not evidence; operator responsibilities for privacy, authority and verification remain.',
 ['Trace a fictional loop over three harmless test items.','Mark its inputs, conditions, outputs and error paths.','Review a generated command against official documentation without executing it.','Create a redaction and approval checklist for AI-assisted support.'],[CMD],['core2-4.10'])

